00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:36.960
community.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.480
YIFEI: It's around the time
when TensorFlow start to really

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.060
grow, like half a year after
we open sourced TensorFlow,

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.480
and we really do need to devote
some engineering power to make

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.370
sure that, with all
these contributions--

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.100
so many people have been so
enthusiastic about contributing

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.110
to TensorFlow-- we
can still guarantee

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.480
the quality and the
productivity of the engineers.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.207
MARK: For those people
who maybe, they're

00:04:56.207 --> 00:04:58.290
new to this space or haven't
heard of this before,

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.380
can you give us the
30,000 foot view

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.790
of what is TensorFlow,
like super high level?

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.260
YIFEI: Oh, yeah, for sure.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.340
So TensorFlow is this
open source machine

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.950
learning platform, library,
however you want to call it,

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:11.250
from Google.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:14.030
And so the way it works,
you are representing

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:18.360
your numerical accretion
with these data flow graphs.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.600
So in these data flow
graphs, your mass operations

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:25.520
will be the node, and your data,
or tensor, will be the edges.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.250
So this kind of
flexible architecture

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:32.030
can allow you to
easily run your models

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:34.910
across all kinds of
platforms, so from CPU,

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:39.110
GPU, to embedded system, to
mobile, to server, and even

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:42.720
to customize hardware and such
as tensor processing units.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.200
And it's also really easy
to scale from research

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:45.996
to production.

00:05:45.996 --> 00:05:47.870
You don't really need
to change the code much

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:50.840
if you want to, say, deploy
your model into production

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:51.680
right away.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.690
MELANIE: In terms of
working with TensorFlow

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:56.120
and, specifically, the
open source community,

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:57.470
I mean, what's it been like?

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.090
What's the community like?

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:03.320
YIFEI: So I'd say the
community is quite inclusive.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:04.970
It's kind of amazing.

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:08.000
No matter what your background
is, no matter you're

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.300
from one of the biggest
company, no matter you're

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.550
from a small company,
no matter you're

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:16.790
here, you're in Asia, Europe,
anywhere else in the world,

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:18.650
and no matter you're
just one individual

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:21.590
working on your own thing, you
can, if you have a great idea,

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:23.660
if there's people
who like what you're

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:26.609
going to bring to the table, you
can contribute to TensorFlow.

00:06:26.609 --> 00:06:28.900
That's kind of the whole
point of open source software,

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:32.040
just anybody with a great idea,
with ability to contribute,

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:33.930
they can make a contribution.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:37.760
And we have definitely accept
contributions across board

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:41.090
from small stuff, like, hey,
fix our documentation errors

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:44.595
to huge changes like
support [INAUDIBLE] or Mac,

00:06:44.595 --> 00:06:47.150
or support Windows binaries.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:49.690
So it's kind of amazing
to be able to see

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:52.160
all these kind of
contribution rolling in

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:53.900
and see the whole
community thrive

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:56.900
because of different changes
different people are making.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:58.608
MELANIE: What are some
of the reasons why

00:06:58.608 --> 00:07:00.820
you think Google made the
decision to open source

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:02.150
TensorFlow to begin with.

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:04.480
I know you came on
board after they did.

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:08.120
YIFEI: I feel like AI has been
playing such an important role

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:11.390
in terms of how technology
has been-- like, progress.

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:14.570
And Google being the
lead in this field,

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:18.620
it's great to open up the
core of its machine learning

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:22.880
and let so many people
to be able to collaborate

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:27.020
and also build on top of
that, or see what we are doing

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:29.250
and be like, hey, I
have this new ideas,

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:31.230
and I want to add
on top of that.

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:33.500
So kind of encourage
collaboration

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:37.680
and make sure we're all moving
towards the good direction

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:38.450
together.

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:40.360
MELANIE: I know being
in the AI community,

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.860
I've seen a lot of open sourcing
over the last few years,

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:43.905
and it's exciting.

00:07:43.905 --> 00:07:46.280
It's a great way to bring
people together to share ideas,

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:49.550
to also speed up the
process and the turnaround

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:53.240
of experimentation and building
up its community as well.

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.480
YIFEI: I think it's going to be
really hard for TensorFlow team

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:58.790
to have the time
or the knowledge

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:01.860
to implement a lot of these
things we have right now,

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.220
and I guess one of
the reasons TensorFlow

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:07.050
is proven to be so
portable and work across

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:09.890
so many platforms is because
we have so many people.

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:11.810
They work on it, and
they've tried it,

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:13.850
and they made sure
that it worked

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:15.736
across all sorts of platforms.

00:08:15.736 --> 00:08:17.860
MARK: So actually, when we
get stuck into sort of--

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:19.880
I know this is part of the stuff
that you've been talking about,

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:21.546
and you mentioned it
came out of Google,

00:08:21.546 --> 00:08:23.090
but it's now open source.

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:28.150
Is there like a magic internal
TensorFlow, or how is that?

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:28.820
How is that?

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:30.680
Because I know we do a
lot of research here.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:33.500
YIFEI: Right, that is a question
a lot of people have asked,

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:35.993
so to a lot of
people's surprise,

00:08:35.993 --> 00:08:37.159
it's actually quite similar.

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:39.179
It's pretty much the same.

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:43.049
So I guess if we're going to
talk about the two versions,

00:08:43.049 --> 00:08:45.470
it's hard to not talk
about this process we have

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:47.540
to sync the Google
version of the code

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:49.850
and the GitHub
version of the code.

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:53.000
So we accept for
[INAUDIBLE] on GitHub,

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.370
as everybody knows,
and meanwhile, we also

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:58.700
accept internal commit
allowing internal repository.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:01.090
And actually, we
have this rotation.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:03.050
One TensorFlow engineer,
every week, they

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:05.630
will be on this rotation,
and every day, they

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:08.780
will push all of the internal
changes out to GitHub.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:10.810
So each internal change
will show up on GitHub

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:13.590
up as a commit, and
at the same time,

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.210
they will go review
GitHub pull request.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:18.090
If it's something
simple, then usually,

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:20.391
we'll just review it, run
the task, and accept it.

00:09:20.391 --> 00:09:21.890
And if it's something
more involved,

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:25.280
they usually will assign it
to a TensorFlow core team

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:28.130
member who may have more
context over the topics,

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:29.210
and they will review it.

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:32.230
And eventually, all these
pull requests like on GitHub.

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:35.230
It's not internal yet,
and about once per week,

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:38.000
we'll also do the same thing,
bring all these GitHub changes

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.380
back to internal
version of TensorFlow.

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:43.550
So we have this
tool that will take

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:46.385
a long list of the differences
between internal and external

00:09:46.385 --> 00:09:48.260
version, and when
I say differences,

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:50.530
it's not really a huge
chunk of differences.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:53.870
It's mostly things like
mechanical pass changes,

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:56.870
or sometimes we will exclude
some directory if it's not

00:09:56.870 --> 00:09:58.070
ready to be released yet.

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:01.070
And sometimes, there is
Google specific dependency

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:03.650
that's just for Google
internal infrastructure, which

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:05.460
wouldn't be useful
outside, and then we

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:08.650
will replace it or remove it for
the open source version of it.

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:11.750
So we run all these transforms
through this tool, which

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:13.120
is actually also open source.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:14.080
It's called Copybara.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:15.880
And then we get
open source version,

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:18.340
and we always make sure that,
if we run the tool backward,

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:19.660
we get the same version back.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:23.650
So it's actually a check that
we ran all the internal changes

00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:26.830
to make sure you always can
create an external version,

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:28.270
don't break anything
open source,

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:29.353
and be able to bring back.

00:10:29.353 --> 00:10:30.980
So they are actually
pretty close.

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:33.702
I'll say a day or two, a
week, apart from each other.

00:10:33.702 --> 00:10:36.160
MARK: So at some point, they--
it's an eventual consistency

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:37.010
model, essentially.

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:37.330
Right?

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:38.163
YIFEI: That's right.

00:10:38.163 --> 00:10:40.230
It's actually quite
challenging to managing this.

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:42.970
You can kind of imagine that
having two source of truth

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:46.891
and given the volume that
we're getting from both side,

00:10:46.891 --> 00:10:49.390
it's very likely that there are
going to be changes touching

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:50.650
the same piece of code.

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:51.800
MELANIE: I can imagine
that would definitely

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:53.500
be one of the bigger
challenges that you're

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.541
dealing with in terms of
dealing with open source

00:10:55.541 --> 00:10:56.375
versus the internal.

00:10:56.375 --> 00:10:58.791
What are some of the other
challenges you find when you're

00:10:58.791 --> 00:10:59.980
working with open source?

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:02.063
YIFEI: I guess that's one
of it, and another thing

00:11:02.063 --> 00:11:05.730
we have is just, we're getting
tons of issues on GitHub.

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:07.240
And so we also
have another thing

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:08.920
that's called the
issue rotation, which

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:09.640
is very similar.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:13.360
So basically, we have somebody
on the team every week that

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:15.444
will go through all the
issues that we're getting,

00:11:15.444 --> 00:11:16.818
and they'll do
something similar.

00:11:16.818 --> 00:11:18.910
If it's something they
can resolve really fast,

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:22.460
they will try to answer, and
if is something more involved,

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:25.570
especially if it's a performance
issue or clearly a bug,

00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:28.580
they will assign it to somebody
who know more about it.

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:30.460
And then somebody
with more expertise

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:32.264
can carry on with
the conversation.

00:11:32.264 --> 00:11:33.680
Or if there are
feature requests--

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:35.370
we're getting tons
of feature requests,

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:36.911
so that's also
something that we want

00:11:36.911 --> 00:11:38.730
to work on to kind of
apply a label to all

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:40.730
these issues [INAUDIBLE]
contribution will come,

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:44.434
so we can get the community
to join in and help us.

00:11:44.434 --> 00:11:45.850
And there are also
people who have

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:46.930
been asking us,
hey, I really want

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:48.310
to contribute to TensorFlow.

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:49.030
What should I do?

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:50.470
And we always
recommend, hey, look

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:52.342
at this list of
Contribution Welcome.

00:11:52.342 --> 00:11:54.050
Pick something that
you're interested in.

00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:55.133
Send us a support request.

00:11:55.133 --> 00:11:55.930
We'll review it.

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:56.980
MARK: So you
mentioned that there

00:11:56.980 --> 00:11:58.063
were two sources of truth.

00:11:58.063 --> 00:11:59.980
So basically, there's
the outer community

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:02.930
and there's Google, who's also
working on this full time.

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:05.330
How do you determine what
should be worked on with what?

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:08.240
Is there one ruling body, or is
it just kind of free for all,

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:09.375
or what happens?

00:12:09.375 --> 00:12:12.250
YIFEI: I think it's more
kind of an organic process.

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:14.970
We don't really have specific
rules of, hey, this feature

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:15.970
needs to be implemented.

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:17.200
These shouldn't be.

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:19.780
[INAUDIBLE] for
any big features,

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:21.351
usually, we will have a plan.

00:12:21.351 --> 00:12:22.600
We'll have [INAUDIBLE] review.

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:24.880
We'll have that
implemented internally.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:27.370
There are also definitely
ideas that we want to do,

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:30.490
but we might not even have
the expertise within Google

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:30.990
to do it.

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:33.489
Somebody else will probably
want it more than we do already,

00:12:33.489 --> 00:12:35.590
and they will give us a
contribution on GitHub.

00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:37.740
They were more than
happy to accept those.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:40.780
And then there are
also smaller features

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:42.970
that we might think about
that is good to have,

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:45.310
but we haven't really
got to them yet.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:47.080
And then if we get
enough momentum

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:49.266
on the community--
from the community,

00:12:49.266 --> 00:12:50.890
there's so many people
asking about it.

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:53.680
Then we're like, OK, maybe
we should prioritize that

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:56.580
better because so many people
really want this feature,

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:57.700
and then we work on those.

00:12:57.700 --> 00:13:00.100
And some other times, there
are also smaller features.

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:02.870
People just directly contribute
to us, and then we're like,

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:04.570
OK, this totally
makes sense to have.

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:07.630
And we haven't had time to
even do something like this,

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:09.190
and great, we'll
take those features.

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:10.570
MARK: So it sounds
like a really--

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:11.825
YIFEI: So it's more
community driven.

00:13:11.825 --> 00:13:12.140
MARK: Yeah, very.

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:13.150
That's what I was going to say.

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:14.800
Sounds like a very strong
community driven process.

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:15.970
That sounds great.

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:17.560
MELANIE: In terms
of TensorFlow, you

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:20.164
were saying before about
how you guys reconcile

00:13:20.164 --> 00:13:21.580
between the internal
and external,

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:24.670
and there's a daily
reconciliation to a degree.

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:26.660
It's not necessarily a release.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:29.140
So what does a release
look like for TensorFlow?

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:30.230
YIFEI: Great question.

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:33.920
So when we talk about this sync
between internal and external

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:35.890
world, this pretty
much all happens

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:38.270
on the master branch of GitHub.

00:13:38.270 --> 00:13:41.560
But for release, we usually
cut a release branch,

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:43.460
and everything happens
on that branch.

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:46.210
So usually, we will
have a list of features

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:49.210
that we know we want to
get into the next release,

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:51.070
and then we cut
a release branch.

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:54.130
We wouldn't want any
new unstable change

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:55.924
to get put into
that branch anymore.

00:13:55.924 --> 00:13:57.340
So that's the
branch where we will

00:13:57.340 --> 00:14:00.340
try to roll out the release
tasks, build our binaries,

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:03.250
and then we will release
an RC version first,

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:04.240
Release Candidates.

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:06.370
Once the release
candidate's out there,

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:09.146
people will try to use
it and let us know,

00:14:09.146 --> 00:14:11.020
hey, if there's an issue,
is there a new bug,

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:13.150
and that's one way we
cherry pick new changes

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:15.590
from the master branch
into the release branch

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:18.100
to fix all these
issues that showed up

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:19.410
in the release candidates.

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:21.910
And sometimes, you will need
to go through a few iterations,

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:25.630
and that's when we eventually
announce the official release.

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:28.150
MELANIE: And I know you
have the release 1.5,

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:31.240
and are there specific
features that you

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:33.880
wanted to highlight with
regards to your release 1.5?

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:37.300
YIFEI: 1.5 have all lot
of our exciting features.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:40.870
Some of them have being in the
preview in the nightly binary

00:14:40.870 --> 00:14:44.920
for a while, but there will
finally be an official release.

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:48.760
So for example, TF Lite is one
of the more exciting thing.

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:50.620
It's TensorFlow's
mobile runtime.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:51.970
MARK: Oh, cool.

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:56.020
YIFEI: Basically, it allows you
to have a really small binary

00:14:56.020 --> 00:14:58.810
size and be able to run
your machine learning

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:00.940
model inference on device.

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:02.560
So that's like two
things you care

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:06.450
a lot when you're trying you
do something on meta platforms.

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:07.080
MELANIE: Yeah.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:09.080
YIFEI: Another thing
that's very exciting-- it's

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:13.040
TF Eager Execution, which I
think was also announced late

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:15.110
last year, just like TF Lite.

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:17.870
Basically, it's the
imperative mode of TensorFlow.

00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:20.150
Earlier, we talked about the
standard TensorFlow works

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:22.580
as a data flow
graph versus Eager,

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:24.700
the code will actually
execute the order

00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:27.275
you're writing your Python
code, which as you can imagine,

00:15:27.275 --> 00:15:29.499
would be great for
debugging or experiment.

00:15:29.499 --> 00:15:31.040
MELANIE: Well, this
is great to hear,

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:33.114
and I know that's always
very exciting when

00:15:33.114 --> 00:15:34.030
there's a new release.

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:36.920
But also, I know you
get a lot of reaction.

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:40.610
People will experiment and
explore and have opinions,

00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:42.650
and I'm sure you see that.

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:44.600
What's it like
managing the reactions

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:46.670
once you do have the release?

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:48.260
YIFEI: I guess for
releases, sometimes

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:50.280
we always need to move forward.

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:52.280
Say, for our GPU
releases, there are going

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:55.310
to be new [INAUDIBLE] version.

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:58.850
We're going to sometimes make
some backward incompatible

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:02.510
changes, so sometimes
this kind of decision

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:05.120
we need to make whether
we want to distribute

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:07.550
our binaries with a
better performance

00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:10.280
or like more people
with older platforms

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:11.852
will still be able to use it.

00:16:11.852 --> 00:16:13.310
It's quite tricky
sometimes to make

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:15.680
that decision of, hey, do we
want to stay with the older

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:18.572
stuff but sacrifice performance
for a lot of people,

00:16:18.572 --> 00:16:20.030
or do we want to
just be like, hey,

00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:22.897
we're just going to move
forward because more people will

00:16:22.897 --> 00:16:24.980
be able to get out of the
box binaries with better

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:25.961
performance?

00:16:25.961 --> 00:16:27.710
But at the end of the
day, there is always

00:16:27.710 --> 00:16:30.050
the source code there,
so you can always

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:31.460
build it from scratch.

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:33.235
MELANIE: You can always play
around with it, or explore--

00:16:33.235 --> 00:16:35.318
YIFEI: Right, you can
always build your own binary

00:16:35.318 --> 00:16:36.950
on the platform that
you want to run.

00:16:36.950 --> 00:16:38.783
It's kind of always a
hard decision to make.

00:16:38.783 --> 00:16:41.210
MELANIE: When you decide
to stop supporting

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:43.520
certain binaries or
certain backgrounds,

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:45.500
how do you communicate
that out usually?

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:47.000
YIFEI: First of
all, when we decide

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:50.360
to move onto a new
version of something,

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:53.810
we will get some requests from
the community a long time ago.

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:56.020
That's where we
start to think about,

00:16:56.020 --> 00:16:58.520
hey, maybe we should do this,
or we know we want to do this,

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:00.830
but that's where we
hear the feedback.

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:03.860
And if more people are willing
to be like, hey, I would rather

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:06.380
update my operating system
or something in order

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:09.500
to get the performance gain,
then we make the decision,

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:11.119
hey, this is
something we should do

00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:13.550
because it sounds like
most of the community

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:16.144
would prefer something like this
versus the other way around.

00:17:16.144 --> 00:17:18.560
MARK: TensorFlow has obviously
become a very, very popular

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:19.069
project.

00:17:19.069 --> 00:17:21.890
Has there been anything you
found particularly surprising

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:24.050
when working with the
open source community?

00:17:24.050 --> 00:17:25.760
Was there a feature that
you thought was, like, well,

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:26.766
I never saw that coming?

00:17:26.766 --> 00:17:28.265
Or was there any
aspect of that that

00:17:28.265 --> 00:17:30.200
you found particularly
surprising or interesting?

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:31.950
YIFEI: A lot of time,
it's very surprising

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:34.280
how many people are
actually working on things,

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:36.710
or how many people have
been paying attention.

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:39.610
For example, before we
had official Windows

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:43.550
binary release, we have been
trying to get it to work.

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:45.590
And when we announced
the intention of, hey,

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:48.050
we're thinking of
releasing a Windows binary,

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:50.595
and I think to our surprise,
Microsoft stepped in

00:17:50.595 --> 00:17:52.920
and be like, hey, we have
been trying to do the same,

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:54.575
and here's some
pull requests that's

00:17:54.575 --> 00:17:57.530
our progress, which was amazing
because it would have taken us

00:17:57.530 --> 00:17:58.400
way longer.

00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:00.590
There are probably lots
of this kind of example.

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:02.227
That's one that I remember.

00:18:02.227 --> 00:18:02.810
MELANIE: Nice.

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:03.800
Nice.

00:18:03.800 --> 00:18:05.270
How large is the community?

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:06.560
YIFEI: It has been growing.

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:10.957
By now, we have about
27k commits on GitHub.

00:18:10.957 --> 00:18:12.290
MELANIE: That's very impressive.

00:18:12.290 --> 00:18:13.040
MARK: I'm seeing--

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:14.300
YIFEI: 12k contributors.

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:15.080
That's a lot.

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:16.345
MARK: 87,000 stars?

00:18:16.345 --> 00:18:16.970
YIFEI: Oh, wow.

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:20.220
And I think we get probably
around 100 issues per week.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:22.220
Probably around the same
number of pull requests

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:24.630
per week as well,
which is a lot.

00:18:24.630 --> 00:18:26.630
MELANIE: I know you were
saying at the beginning

00:18:26.630 --> 00:18:30.560
that you find it really exciting
how people build on this, ideas

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:33.380
that they come up with in
terms the way they contribute.

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:36.080
What makes you so
excited, outside of that,

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:37.790
about working on open source?

00:18:37.790 --> 00:18:42.200
YIFEI: I think it's a great
learning experience because you

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:45.830
get to see how people
approach the same problem

00:18:45.830 --> 00:18:47.370
in different ways.

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:50.330
You see solutions that-- the way
to attack a problem you'd never

00:18:50.330 --> 00:18:51.860
even think of.

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:55.430
And you interact with, pretty
much, the experts of fields.

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:57.080
And I guess it's also
about the people.

00:18:57.080 --> 00:18:58.950
It's always exciting
to go to a conference

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:00.950
and just run into somebody
that's like, oh, hey,

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:03.920
I remember you from
a pull request,

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:05.120
which is always exciting.

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:06.036
MELANIE: This is cool.

00:19:06.036 --> 00:19:08.390
This is cool that you have
so much excitement out

00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:09.890
of working with the community.

00:19:09.890 --> 00:19:11.000
MARK: So this all
sounds really cool,

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:12.830
and its great to hear your
passion for open source

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:14.180
and the TensorFlow community.

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:16.804
If people are listening in, and
they're saying themselves, man,

00:19:16.804 --> 00:19:19.220
I really wish I could get
involved with the TensorFlow

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:21.270
community, what's a
great place to start?

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:22.940
I think you touched on this
a little bit previously,

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:24.680
but I think if we can give
people some concrete examples,

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:25.400
that would be great.

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:27.733
YIFEI: As I mentioned earlier,
one of the greatest place

00:19:27.733 --> 00:19:30.740
to start if you want to
contribute code to TensorFlow

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:33.380
is to go TensorFlow's
issue page,

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:36.140
and for everything that
we've liked the community

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:39.980
to contribute, we'll tag with
Contribution Welcome label,

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:42.360
and you can go through those
issues and be like, hey,

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:44.110
this is something
that's very interesting.

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:46.290
Oh, this is really relevant
to what I'm working on,

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.764
and I can probably make some
contribution on this field.

00:19:48.764 --> 00:19:50.180
And that's one
place that's great,

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:52.790
and another place
is Stack Overflow.

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:55.790
So we have thousands,
thousands [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:55.790 --> 00:19:58.280
tagged with TensorFlow
on Stack Overflow,

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:00.111
and it would be
great if somebody

00:20:00.111 --> 00:20:01.610
can take a look at
them and be like,

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:03.200
oh, hey, I know the
answer to these questions.

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:04.620
I ran into the same issues.

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:06.600
MELANIE: I think that's
a great example, too,

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:08.670
because there's also
the people out there

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:10.010
who might want to be involved.

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:11.718
They've never done an
open source before,

00:20:11.718 --> 00:20:13.236
and they're intimidated by it.

00:20:13.236 --> 00:20:14.610
And they don't
think, oh, there's

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:16.100
no way I could solve
these kind of problems

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:18.433
or there's no way I could
contribute to something as big

00:20:18.433 --> 00:20:19.440
as TensorFlow.

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:21.930
So Stack Overflow--

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:23.412
YIFEI: It's a great
place to start.

00:20:23.412 --> 00:20:24.870
MELANIE: Especially
smaller issues,

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:26.680
tackling smaller issues.

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:29.310
Do you have any recommendations
for best practices

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:32.087
or ways people should think
about engaging, especially

00:20:32.087 --> 00:20:33.420
if they've never done it before?

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:34.961
They've done very
little, and they're

00:20:34.961 --> 00:20:36.810
very intimidated by this?

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:40.500
YIFEI: I guess it's kind of
similar rules for contributing

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:42.880
to pretty much any
open source projects.

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:46.140
So always do your homework,
understanding the rules for how

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:48.370
to contribute to a project.

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:50.970
So for example, on
TensorFlow's GitHub main page,

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:54.720
there is contributing
documentation, kind of list

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:57.000
out what are the
rules that you should

00:20:57.000 --> 00:20:59.130
follow when you contribute.

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:00.680
And then there are
things like when

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:03.390
you are going to
add a new feature,

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:06.060
before you send like
a huge pull request,

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:10.140
it might be good to start an
issue to talk to the maintainer

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:13.590
just to see, hey, if
something that I'm proposing

00:21:13.590 --> 00:21:15.660
is going to align with
the general direction

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:18.840
of the project, or even
sometimes, somebody

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:22.290
might already be working on
something similar internally.

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:26.790
And it's always good to
just check up on, hey,

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:28.166
I want to do
something like this.

00:21:28.166 --> 00:21:28.915
What do you think?

00:21:28.915 --> 00:21:30.360
Do you guys think
it's reasonable?

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:31.500
MELANIE: Is that the
best way, though,

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:33.610
putting an issue in to be able
to get in touch with people?

00:21:33.610 --> 00:21:35.650
YIFEI: So if it's something that
hasn't been brought up before,

00:21:35.650 --> 00:21:38.490
then there are also a lot of
opportunities on the issue

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:41.360
page, where you can filter
through by the Contribution

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:42.729
Welcome label.

00:21:42.729 --> 00:21:45.270
And it's also good to just leave
a message, and be like, hey,

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:47.245
I'm thinking of doing this.

00:21:47.245 --> 00:21:48.120
What do you guys say?

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:50.280
And everybody's like, oh,
hey, thumbs up, go ahead.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:51.990
And then you can
send a pull request

00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:54.531
because sometimes there might
be something tricky that you're

00:21:54.531 --> 00:21:57.210
not aware of, and maybe
somebody on the team or somebody

00:21:57.210 --> 00:21:58.890
proposing the feature
from the community

00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:00.600
can give you some
guidance on that.

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:02.891
MELANIE: Do you guys have
any chat channels or anything

00:22:02.891 --> 00:22:04.669
that people hang out
in and share ideas?

00:22:04.669 --> 00:22:05.460
YIFEI: Not so much.

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:06.900
I think, in terms
of that, usually

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:11.480
we just use GitHub Issues so
people with command on issues.

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:14.160
If somebody likes idea, they
might sum up the comments

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:14.670
or something like that.

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:16.440
MARK: And TensorFlow has
a contributing guideline

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:17.497
that people can go read?

00:22:17.497 --> 00:22:18.330
YIFEI: That's right.

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:20.315
So that's on the GitHub page.

00:22:20.315 --> 00:22:21.690
MELANIE: Anything
that you wanted

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:25.290
to mention before we wrap this
up, in terms of open source,

00:22:25.290 --> 00:22:28.099
TensorFlow, working with
these types of communities?

00:22:28.099 --> 00:22:29.640
YIFEI: I guess this
is also something

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:32.580
that's more for a general
contributing to open source.

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:36.019
It's really important, I guess,
to be respectful and mindful

00:22:36.019 --> 00:22:37.560
because a lot of
time, people forget,

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:40.350
hey, there's a human
behind the computer,

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:42.940
so everybody contributing
to a project,

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:45.120
coming from such
different backgrounds,

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:47.800
and the way they approach
problem can be very different.

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:51.000
So you kind of always need
to be positive and open

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:54.720
minded to be ready to try to
understand where other people's

00:22:54.720 --> 00:23:00.120
opinions are from and try
to explain your opinions,

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:01.710
so if there's any
disagreement, always

00:23:01.710 --> 00:23:04.890
kind of be willing to
go in with an open mind.

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:06.510
Try to understand
what's going on.

00:23:06.510 --> 00:23:07.590
Try to use all of it.

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:09.140
I think that helps,
when everybody

00:23:09.140 --> 00:23:11.971
is in a positive mindset, even
if there are a lot of conflicts

00:23:11.971 --> 00:23:13.470
on this pull request,
and people are

00:23:13.470 --> 00:23:14.928
going to be working
together trying

00:23:14.928 --> 00:23:17.692
to figure things out and
get you the best solution.

00:23:17.692 --> 00:23:18.900
So that's something I think--

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:21.480
I find it very important
to encourage more people

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:23.040
to contribute to the community.

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:24.040
MELANIE: I agree.

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:26.430
I think it's great to get a
variety of inputs, especially

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:27.763
across the board in a community.

00:23:27.763 --> 00:23:28.570
It's very helpful.

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:29.107
Well, great.

